{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788474115-e7uuIGIc1hZwHRLsBvDl","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me look at the existing tests to understand how they test these middlewares, and understand how the auth_test response headers work. Let me check how `MultiTeamsAuthorization` gets scopes — via the installation store. For SingleTeamAuthorization, the auth.test call result includes headers like \"x-oauth-scopes\" — actually Slack's auth.test response includes headers `x-accept-oauth-scopes` and `x-oauth-scopes`.\n\nLet me check the SlackResponse structure — headers are available in `SlackResponse.headers`. The `x-oauth-scopes` header contains the bot token scopes; `x-accept-oauth-scopes` is about what the method accepts. For user tokens, the header would be... Let me think. In Slack API, auth.test response headers:\n- `x-accept-oauth-scopes`: scopes accepted by this method\n- `x-oauth-scopes`: scopes the current token has\n\nFor user tokens (xoxp), `x-oauth-scopes` would include the user token scopes.\n\nSo in `_to_authorize_result`, we need to extract headers from the auth_test_result: `auth_test_result.headers.get(\"x-oauth-scopes\")`. Headers in slack_sdk's SlackResponse are a dict of lists (case-insensitive? headers are stored as returned by urllib3/httpx). Let me check how slack_sdk stores headers.\n\nLet me look at the existing tests to see the mocked auth_test responses.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Parasail","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02e22cc0-e01a-43f1-b698-3e20536e0a1c","uuid":"08cbdffe-8ec4-4938-b55b-a6f4e5af8ffd",