"),\n        bot_user_id=user_id if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\n        bot_token=token if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\n        user_id=request_user_id or (user_id if not _is_bot_token(token) else None),\n        user_token=token if not _is_bot_token(token) else None,\n        bot_scopes=_parse_scopes_header_value(auth_test_result.headers.get(\"x-oauth-scopes\")) if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\n        user_scopes=... if not _is_bot_token(token) else None,\n    )\n```\n\nHmm, let me actually check the real upstream implementation. I remember in bolt-python's internals.py (current master):\n\n```python\ndef _read_scopes_from_response_headers(auth_test_result) -> Optional[Union[List[str], str]]:\n    headers = auth_test_result.headers or {}\n    raw_value: Optional[str] = headers.get(\"x-oauth-scopes\")\n    ...\n```\n\nI can't fetch the upstream repo easily... actually I can try WebFetch to look at the current bolt-python source. Let me try fetching the file from GitHub. That would help match upstream behavior exactly. But maybe not necessary — I can implement it robustly myself. Still, matching upstream is nice for compatibility. Let me try.\n\nActually, let me check the git log to see if there are any hints. The task says: \"When processing an authorization request, I want the middleware to extract the OAuth scopes from the response headers (using the \"x-oauth-scopes\" header) and properly assign them based on the token type.\"\n\nSo the implementation:\n1. In `_to_authorize_result` (shared by both sync and async SingleTeamAuthorization middlewares), extract scopes from `auth_test_result.headers` \"x-oauth-scopes\".\n2. Assign to `bot_scopes` if token is bot token (xoxb-), else to `user_scopes`.\n\nThe AuthorizeResult constructor already handles comma-separated string → list conversion for bot_scopes/user_scopes. \n\nLet me check upstream to be safe.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":